WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.240
The first film fixed the codon boxes.

2
00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:05.640
How does each box choose an amino acid?

3
00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:10.480
Keep the same blue message, gold start, and teal reading window.

4
00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:14.720
Now add the adaptor that connects sequence to protein.

5
00:00:18.233 --> 00:00:20.873
A transfer RNA has two working ends.

6
00:00:22.073 --> 00:00:24.193
Its anticodon can pair with a codon.

7
00:00:25.393 --> 00:00:27.693
Its other end carries an amino acid.

8
00:00:28.993 --> 00:00:32.413
The adaptor keeps both identities physically linked as it moves.

9
00:00:33.467 --> 00:00:39.587
Before the adaptor reaches a ribosome, an aminoacyl transfer RNA synthetase usually

10
00:00:39.587 --> 00:00:46.287
loads it. The enzyme selects an amino acid and the transfer RNA meant to carry it.

11
00:00:47.387 --> 00:00:49.607
Some synthetases also remove mistakes.

12
00:00:50.717 --> 00:00:53.697
Translation therefore depends on separate checks.

13
00:00:54.977 --> 00:00:56.817
The synthetase chooses the cargo.

14
00:00:58.097 --> 00:01:00.937
At the ribosome, the anticodon tests the codon.

15
00:01:02.077 --> 00:01:06.597
The growing chain receives whatever cargo arrived on the accepted adaptor.

16
00:01:06.597 --> 00:01:11.837
Charged transfer RNAs connect codons to the growing protein.

17
00:01:12.950 --> 00:01:14.290
Predict this case.

18
00:01:15.530 --> 00:01:21.190
A transfer RNA carries the wrong amino acid, but its anticodon matches the codon.

19
00:01:22.410 --> 00:01:25.250
Will the ribosome reject the adaptor, or accept it?

20
00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:32.560
The pairing check passes.

21
00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:36.860
The ribosome does not inspect the amino acid itself.

22
00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:39.960
The wrong cargo can enter the chain.

23
00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:45.640
Accuracy therefore depends on charging before the ribosome and pairing at the

24
00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:46.640
ribosome.

25
00:01:47.233 --> 00:01:48.613
Now follow a cycle.

26
00:01:50.013 --> 00:01:53.653
The P site holds the transfer RNA carrying the growing chain.

27
00:01:54.973 --> 00:01:59.993
The A site receives a charged adaptor whose anticodon matches the next codon.

28
00:02:01.133 --> 00:02:02.553
The E site is the exit.

29
00:02:03.617 --> 00:02:08.757
The large subunit transfers the chain onto the amino acid in the A site.

30
00:02:10.017 --> 00:02:12.337
Then the ribosome advances by a codon.

31
00:02:13.517 --> 00:02:18.317
The chain-bearing adaptor moves into the P site, while the empty adaptor reaches the

32
00:02:18.317 --> 00:02:19.397
E site and leaves.

33
00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:22.780
The ribosome is not merely a track.

34
00:02:24.020 --> 00:02:30.880
Its large-subunit RNA positions the two transfer RNA ends and catalyzes peptide-bond

35
00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:35.340
formation. The reaction occurs in an RNA-lined pocket.

36
00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:39.020
That is why the ribosome is called a ribozyme.

37
00:02:40.083 --> 00:02:43.023
The same blue ribbon moves through the teal window.

38
00:02:44.383 --> 00:02:49.183
Each accepted adaptor adds a green block, so the chain grows in codon order.

39
00:02:50.483 --> 00:02:53.703
The ribosome repeats the cycle without changing the frame.

40
00:02:54.767 --> 00:03:00.127
When a stop enters the A site, a release factor arrives instead of a transfer RNA.

41
00:03:01.447 --> 00:03:05.027
The completed chain leaves, and the ribosome can be recycled.

42
00:03:06.287 --> 00:03:08.107
The stop adds no amino acid.

43
00:03:09.100 --> 00:03:12.460
The codon never touches its amino acid directly.

44
00:03:13.740 --> 00:03:18.060
A charged adaptor makes the connection, while the ribosome preserves the order.

45
00:03:19.340 --> 00:03:22.360
The adaptor carries identity from the code to the chain.
